# Who has to look at a change before it lands.
#
# This file used to name one path, on the argument that a CODEOWNERS naming an
# owner for every directory turns review into a formality nobody reads. That
# argument still holds and this list is still short: what has been added is not
# "the core", it is the twelve paths measured as the ones app work drags along
# with it. Of the last 300 commits, 133 touched
# backend/internal/apps; the files below are the ones they touched at the same
# time, most often. Every one of them is a place where an app's change lands in
# the platform because the platform gave it nowhere else to go.
#
# Being here does not mean "do not change this". It means the change is worth a
# second pair of eyes asking whether it belongs in the core at all.
#
# Owner: @erdenebatt. A person rather than a team, and the reason is worth
# writing down because the obvious fix does not work.
#
# A team was tried. @gerege-systems/core-team held no write access, and GitHub
# resolves an owner it cannot write to as no owner at all — every line was an
# `Unknown owner` error. Check with
#
#	gh api repos/gerege-systems/open-gerege-nexus/codeowners/errors
#
# Granting the team access made those errors go away and revealed the second
# problem: the team's other member was an account with no commits here and no
# activity anywhere since 2020. It was removed and the team with it.
#
# So a person, and this file is a request rather than a gate. GitHub never asks
# a pull request's own author to review it, and 615 of this repository's commits
# are the owner's, so on a branch pushed from here it requests nothing. Where it
# works is the case this repository is open for: somebody else's pull request
# now reaches the owner by name instead of by whoever happens to look.
#
# Making it a gate needs a second reviewer, not a settings change. A sole owner
# who is also the author is a requirement that can never be satisfied — the only
# way through would be an admin bypass on every pull request, which teaches
# everybody to bypass. When there are two people who read this code, put them in
# a team, give the team write access, and turn on required code-owner reviews.
#
# What does gate merges is the thirteen required status checks on main, and as
# of 2026-08-21 they bind on administrators too — which matters here, because
# this organisation has ten owners and an organisation owner is an administrator
# of every repository in it. Among those checks are the golden files
# (testdata/api.txt, testdata/routes.txt) and the two downstream distribution
# builds. This file says who to ask; those say what changed.

# The ecosystem's contract. Every distribution repository compiles against this
# package and pins it by tag, so a change here is a change to code that is not
# in this repository and cannot be fixed from it — see docs/MODULES.md.
#
# The golden file beside it is part of the same review: its diff is the plain
# statement of what the contract gained or lost.
/backend/pkg/nexus/ @erdenebatt

# The rest of the published surface: the app manifest schema every catalogue
# entry is validated against, and the entry point a distribution's main.go
# calls. The Өртөө wire contract was here too, until the channel left with its
# app for client-gerege-nexus.
/backend/pkg/catalog/ @erdenebatt
/backend/pkg/host/ @erdenebatt

# The bootstrap that builds every module (its signature went from 4 parameters
# to 9 in eleven days): where an app's need for one more line becomes the
# platform's permanent surface. The route table is /backend/pkg/host/ above.
/backend/internal/apps/runtime.go @erdenebatt

# What a tenant gets when it installs an app: which permissions exist and which
# system roles they reach by default (12 commits). A line here changes who can
# do what, in every deployment, and no test failure looks like it.
/backend/internal/workspace/appinstall/ @erdenebatt

# The platform's schema. 104 tables, and the row-level policies that are the
# whole of tenant isolation. An app's table added here is an app's table the
# core now carries forever.
/backend/db/migrations/ @erdenebatt

# The frontend's three shared files, and the three at the top of §2.1's table:
# the API client (40 commits), the translation dictionary that every app is
# spliced into by hand (its locale files, 21 commits each), and the shell that
# names each app's icon and position in a list in its own source.
/frontend/lib/api.ts @erdenebatt
/frontend/lib/i18n/index.tsx @erdenebatt
/frontend/components/Layout.tsx @erdenebatt

# The bundled catalogue (20 commits). What this binary claims it can install.
/catalog/ @erdenebatt
